      "notes": "jq 1.8 semantics on the shared gojq engine: input key order and unmodified number literals are kept (2.0, 1E+3), keys sorts, keys_unsorted and to_entries follow input order, and runtime errors use jq's wording with \"jq: error (at FILE:LINE)\". --arg/--argjson/--slurpfile/--rawfile consume TWO argv words; --slurpfile and --rawfile files are captured and pinned. -s array, -n one null input, -R lines as strings (-Rs one string); -r and -j print strings raw (-j without newlines) unless -a escapes; the last of -c, --tab and --indent N (0-7) sets the layout; -S sorts output keys; -M is accepted (no colour is ever produced). $ENV and env read the program's exported environment; no import/module/filesystem loader; query work/depth/output bounded. A plain redirect re-serializes; jq-edit preserves comments and layout. Without a filter jq runs \".\"; -f reads the filter from a pinned file; operands after --args/--jsonargs are $ARGS.positional, and $ARGS.named holds every named value. --stream evaluates each input's tostream events; --yaml-input/--yaml-output are gojq's YAML spellings. Command-line errors print jq's message and help pointer with status 2.",
